        "original_id": "7d32447e-e7d8-5a79-bf6c-59b03253824a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Loch Shiel, River Shiel, Loch Moidart, Castle Tioram, Eilean Tioram, Dalelia, Dalilea, Acharacle, Fort William, Lochaber, Highland, Scotland, Argyll, Scottish Highlands\n- Concepts: geography, location, Scottish Gaelic names\n\nFacts:\n- Loch Shiel changes considerably along its length, with a deep, mountain-enclosed north-east end and a shallow south-west end surrounded by bog and rough pasture.\n- The 4 km River Shiel drains from the south-west end of Loch Shiel to the sea in Loch Moidart near Castle Tioram.\n- Castle Tioram is a ruined castle on the tidal island Eilean Tioram in Loch Moidart, Lochaber, Highland, Scotland.\n- Castle Tioram is west of Acharacle and about 80 km from Fort William.\n- Dalelia, or Dalilea, is a collection of houses on the north shore of Loch Shiel in the Acharacle district of Argyll, Scottish Highlands, within the Highland council area.\n- Dalelia derives from the Scottish Gaelic name “Dàil Eileadh,” and Castle Tioram derives from the Scottish Gaelic name “Caisteal Tioram,” meaning “dry castle.”",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
